systems from scratch - credits and notices
==========================================

Each track is backed by real, widely used code or formats. This file says,
per track, what was taken from where and under which licence, and what is
the course's own. The site shows the same at /credits.

The course's own work is under the MIT licence: see LICENSE.


xv6 - A Unix kernel for RISC-V (tracks/xv6)
-------------------------------------------

Upstream   xv6 for RISC-V, MIT PDOS
           https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv (branch riscv)
Licence    MIT. Copyright (c) 2006-2024 Frans Kaashoek, Robert Morris,
           Russ Cox, Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
           The full text is in tracks/xv6/LICENSE, which comes with the
           project's files at every lesson.
Taken      The kernel (kernel/), the user programs and user library (user/)
           and mkfs (mkfs/): 63 of the track's files start from the xv6
           file of the same name, most of them with the code nearly as
           xv6 has it, re-commented as lesson notes and cut into lessons.
           user/usertests.c is a smaller suite in the style of xv6's:
           15 tests, 7 of them after xv6's own.
Ours       The lessons, the order the code is built in and its markers,
           the Makefile and README.md, the network extension (module 08: kernel/ether.c,
           ip.c, sock.c, virtio_net.c, net.h, user/nslookup.c, udpecho.c),
           the GUI extension (module 09: kernel/fb.c, fb.h, virtio_input.c,
           input.h, user/gui.c, gui.h, font.c, paint.c, colors.c,
           events.c) and the internal checks (kernel/selftest.c).

Tests      xv6's own user/usertests.c, from the same repository at commit
           7e95e76a19a7168c8db63f3ea40c6641f9ead93e (2025-08-04)
Licence    MIT, as above (tracks/xv6/LICENSE).
Taken      That one file, in tracks/xv6/tests/upstream/, for the internal
           tests only; the site does not show it. Four regression tests
           from later commits of the same repository are backported into
           it (pipezero, killzero, partial_write, unlinkcwd); README.md
           there lists them and says how it is built and run.


db - A SQL database in C (tracks/db)
------------------------------------

Upstream   SQLite, https://sqlite.org/src
           (GitHub mirror: https://github.com/sqlite/sqlite)
Licence    Public domain (https://sqlite.org/copyright.html)
Taken      No code. The engine follows SQLite's documented database file
           format, rollback journal and write-ahead log
           (https://www.sqlite.org/fileformat2.html), and is checked
           against the real sqlite3 program: each opens what the other
           writes, and the same SQL gives the same answers.
Ours       All of the engine (tracks/db/src), written from scratch in C.

Tests      sqllogictest, SQLite's query test corpus, by D. Richard Hipp
           https://www.sqlite.org/sqllogictest/ (GitHub mirror:
           https://github.com/gregrahn/sqllogictest, commit c93ac416f056)
Licence    Copyright (c) 2008 D. Richard Hipp. "You can redistribute it
           and/or modify the code as you see fit. No attribution is
           required", under whichever of the GPL, BSD, MIT or CC0
           licences best applies. The full text is in
           tracks/db/tests/sqllogictest/COPYRIGHT.md.
Taken      15 of its test scripts, unchanged, in
           tracks/db/tests/sqllogictest/test/ (13.9 MB), for the
           internal tests only; the site does not show them. README.md
           there lists them.


