dsa - Data structures and algorithms (tracks/dsa)
-------------------------------------------------

Upstream   None. The tools are written from the documented behaviour of
           the ones they copy and checked against the machine's own: GNU
           coreutils on Linux (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/,
           GPL-3.0-or-later) and the FreeBSD utilities macOS ships
           (https://github.com/freebsd/freebsd-src, BSD licences). No
           code from either is in the track.
Ours       All of the code (lib/, tools/, bench/, data/gen.py, the
           Makefile and the tool.mk files), the lessons and the internal
           checks (tracks/dsa/track.py and tests/).

Data       data/book.txt: The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes, by Arthur
           Conan Doyle (first published 1892; he died in 1930). In the
           public domain in the United States, and wherever copyright
           lasts the author's life and 70 years or less; elsewhere, check
           the law where you are.
Source     Project Gutenberg eBook #1661 (released 1999-03-01, updated
           2023-10-10), https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1661, as plain
           text: https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1661/pg1661.txt,
           607606 bytes, SHA-256 922e2a12ccb43a4c9544c260b2166c6a
           d2097aeb5957faeee113f173bb857cd0, fetched 2026-11-14.
Licence    The book is public domain. Project Gutenberg's licence (its
           section 1.C) claims no right to stop anyone in the United
           States copying or adapting a public-domain work "as long as all
           references to Project Gutenberg are removed", and its trademark
           terms apply only to copies that keep the name. So the file is
           the book alone: the text between the eBook's START and END
           lines, which names Project Gutenberg nowhere, without the
           header, footer and licence around it. Changed: LF line
           endings, and its 6826 non-ASCII characters written in ASCII
           (curly quotes as straight ones, the em dash as --, the pound
           sign as L, accented letters without their accents, ae and oe
           for the ligatures, 1/2 for the half). 562404 bytes, SHA-256
           8d42be2e0715fc0c857e4565e9c59a386ed0dad2c6ae37a2650b3b298109958a.
Generated  Every larger input is made by data/gen.py, with a fixed seed,
           from the book's words and words it makes up; none is stored.

Data       tools/spell/words.txt: the 2of4brif list of Alan Beale's
           12dicts word lists, 60388 English words, unchanged.
Source     https://github.com/en-wl/wordlist (alt12dicts/2of4brif.txt,
           commit b22230cc5250887737fdefe9ca4c9d9d01230eaa), which says
           the file is identical to the one in the 12dicts package
           (http://wordlist.aspell.net/12dicts/).
Licence    Public domain: "I explicitly release them to the public
           domain, but request acknowledgment of their use" (the 12dicts
           readme; the two lists it excepts are not this one). The word
           list is the work of Alan Beale, and this is that
           acknowledgment. tracks/dsa/tools/spell/NOTICE.txt has the
           hashes and the full text as read.

Data       tools/route/london.map: the roads of central London, 17260
           junctions and 22498 roads, as plain text. Map data (c)
           OpenStreetMap contributors,
           https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
Source     OpenStreetMap, fetched once from the Overpass API on
           2026-11-14 and reduced by tools/route/gen.py, which documents
           every step and makes the same bytes again.
Licence    Open Data Commons Open Database License 1.0 (ODbL),
           https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/. london.map is
           a Derivative Database of OpenStreetMap, so it is offered under
           the ODbL and NOT under this repository's MIT licence. Every
           picture route draws from it carries the credit line.
           tracks/dsa/tools/route/NOTICE.txt has the query, the bounds,
           the hashes, and how each term of the licence is met.
           tools/route/tiny.map is made up, and is the course's own.


